# 8.3.6 Multiple regression

This notebook directly ports the three nested testosterone models in section 8.3.6 of [*Biological Data Science with R*, Essential statistics](https://bdsr.stephenturner.us/stats.html). Sections 8.3.4 and 8.3.5 remain available as the separate source-faithful group-comparison and regression lessons.

> **Understanding multiple regression**
>
> **Use it when:** you need to estimate one predictor's association with a numeric outcome while accounting for other measured predictors.
>
> **How to read it:** each coefficient is conditional on the other included terms. A coefficient changing after adjustment can reveal confounding, but adjustment alone does not establish causation.

```biolang
let nh = read_csv("nhanes.csv")
let adults = nh |> filter(|row| row.Age >= 18)
let testosterone_rows = adults |> drop_null("Testosterone") |> drop_null("PhysActive")
let testosterone_no = testosterone_rows |> filter(|row| row.PhysActive == "No") |> col("Testosterone")
let testosterone_yes = testosterone_rows |> filter(|row| row.PhysActive == "Yes") |> col("Testosterone")
let activity_t_test = ttest(testosterone_no, testosterone_yes, {variance: "equal"})
activity_t_test
```

The source reports (t=-2.4298), 3436 degrees of freedom, and (p=0.01516). The matching one-predictor linear model follows.

```biolang
let testosterone = adults |> col("Testosterone")
let activity_model = stats_multiple_linear_diagnostics(
    table({PhysActive: adults |> col("PhysActive")}), testosterone,
    {validation_folds: 0, include_values: false}
)
activity_model.coefficients
```

Adding age removes the physical-activity association at the 0.05 threshold in the source model.

```biolang
let activity_age_model = stats_multiple_linear_diagnostics(
    table({
        PhysActive: adults |> col("PhysActive"),
        Age: adults |> col("Age")
    }), testosterone,
    {validation_folds: 0, include_values: false}
)
activity_age_model.coefficients
```

Adding gender substantially reduces residual variation; the physical-activity coefficient is again significant. The labels use BioLang's explicit bracket notation for treatment contrasts, so `Gender[male]` and `PhysActive[Yes]` are the source's `Gendermale` and `PhysActiveYes` terms.

```biolang
let full_model = stats_multiple_linear_diagnostics(
    table({
        PhysActive: adults |> col("PhysActive"),
        Age: adults |> col("Age"),
        Gender: adults |> col("Gender")
    }), testosterone,
    {validation_folds: 0, include_values: false}
)
{
    coefficients: full_model.coefficients,
    complete_rows: full_model.complete_rows,
    excluded_rows: full_model.excluded_rows,
    r_squared: full_model.r_squared,
    adjusted_r_squared: full_model.adjusted_r_squared
}
```

```biolang
assert abs(activity_t_test.statistic - -2.429815439145285) < 0.000000001, "source activity t statistic"
assert activity_t_test.df == 3436, "source activity t degrees of freedom"
assert abs(activity_model.coefficients[1].estimate - 19.267018611090293) < 0.000000001, "source activity coefficient"
assert abs(activity_age_model.coefficients[1].estimate - -0.8002814918848092) < 0.000000001, "source age coefficient"
assert abs(full_model.coefficients[1].estimate - -0.5903616430454974) < 0.000000001, "source adjusted age coefficient"
assert abs(full_model.coefficients[2].estimate - 385.1989414002692) < 0.000000001, "source male coefficient"
assert abs(full_model.coefficients[3].estimate - 9.274924731642368) < 0.000000001, "source adjusted activity coefficient"
assert full_model.complete_rows == 3438 && full_model.excluded_rows == 269, "source complete cases"
assert abs(full_model.r_squared - 0.6968929855756988) < 0.000000001, "source multiple R squared"
"Exact source calculations passed."
```

